4501:
** ῥομφαία**, ἡ,
**large, broad sword**, used by the Thracians, ὀρθὰς ῥ. βαρυσιδήρους ἀπὸ τῶν δεξιῶν ὤμων ἐπισείοντες {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 18, compare 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus “fragment” 57 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - FHG” 103J.}}: generally, **sword**, {{LXX+NT+4th c.AD+|LXX.Gen.3.24, +others, NT.Luke.2.35, NT.Rev.6.8, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 89b}}; of the **sword** of Goliath, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.1Ki.17.51, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.12.4.}}
**II** ={νυκτερίς}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cyranus 36.}}

4505:
** ῥύμ-η** [ῡ], ἡ,
**force, swing, rush** of a body in motion, ῥύμῃ ἐμπίπτειν with **a swing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76}}; πτερύγων ῥύμη **rush** of wings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 86, compare “Av.” 1182}}; τροχοῦ ῥύμαισι τευκτὸν.. κύτος formed by the **whirl** of the potter's wheel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 52.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 4}}; ἡ ῥ. τοῦ αἵματος the **flow** of blood in the veins, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἰητροῦ” 6}}; ἡ ῥ. τῆς ἐκκρούσεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.12}}; τῆς ῥ. τῆς ἁλιάδος ὁ ψόφος, of the noise made by a boat in motion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 533b19: _metaphorically_, εὐτυχεῖ ῥύμῃ θεοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 64; ἡ ῥ. τῆς τύχης 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 53}}; ἡ ῥ. τῆς ὀργῆς κτλ. the **vehemence** of passion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.99; γλώσσης ῥ. “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 35.11, compare 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.245 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**2** _absolutely_, **rush, charge**, of soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.81}}; of ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.70; τῶν ἵππων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.31, compare 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 4.3.5, +others, 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.12}}; ὑπὸ τοῦ ῥοίβδου καὶ τῆς ῥ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 407.}}
**3** ={ῥοπή}, Chrysipp. cited in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia T 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.212.}}
**II** **street**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 2.5, 3.4; τὴν ῥ. ὁδοιπορεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 13, cf. LXX.Isa.15.3, +others}}; of a Roman camp, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.29.1}}; **lane, alley,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 764.142 (3rd c.BC), “PStrassb.” 86.19 (2nd c.BC), NT.Act.9.11}}; **slit, chink**, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ καρδίης” 2.}}

4507:
** ῥῠπᾰρ-ία**, ἡ,
**dirt, filth**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.56 (probably in 5.74, _plural_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.142a, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.122, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.42.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **sordidness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 56 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus pp.33,37 H., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.60d; οἰκονομικὸς χωρὶς ῥυπαρίας 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 74.5.}}

4508:
** ῥῠπᾰρός**, ά, όν,
**filthy, dirty**, κόλυθρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 3; δάπιδες 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 185}}; ῥ. εἴριον **greasy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 21; γαστέρας [prev. author] “Prorrh.” 2.23 (assuming variant)}}; **unpurged**, σῶμα {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus “de febribus” 7.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **sordid, mean**, ἤδη χορηγὸν πώποτε -ώτερον τοῦδ᾽ εἶδε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 306}}; **uncultured**, ῥ. τρόποι [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|{5-6th c.BC}: Philetaerus Comicus 18}}]; βίος δουλοπρεπὴς καὶ ῥ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Virtutibus et Vitiis” 1251b13}}; ῥ. πολῖται, ὄχλος, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.8, 9.44}}; of style, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 43.5}}. _adverb_ **-ρῶς**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 142, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 43, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.9.4, “Anthologia Graeca” 10.48 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus)}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατα**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 59.4.}}
**3** of coins, **made of base metal, gold** or **silver alloy**, ἀργυρίου ῥυπαρὰς δραχμάς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 214.12 (2nd c.AD),compare “Ostr.Bodl.” ii 32 (2nd c.AD), +others}}
**4** κροτὼν ῥ., probably ={ἀδειγμάτιστος}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 670.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; σῖτος ῥ. **unwinnowed,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|PFay. 16.10 (1st c.BC)}}; κριθὴ ῥ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1542.7 (4th c.AD).}}

4509:
** ῥύπος** [ῠ], ὁ,
**dirt, filth**, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} once in irregular _plural_, κάθηράν τε ῥύπα πάντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.93}}: later in _singular_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.63, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 82 (compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 892), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 130c, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 252, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 197, etc.}}; ὁ ἐν τοῖς ὠσὶ ῥ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 960b18, compare 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.24, “PMag.Osl.” 1.332; ῥ. ὁ ἀπὸ τῶν ἐν τοῖς γυμνασίοις ἀνδριάντων 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.116; τὸν ἀπὸ τῶν ὀνύχων ῥύπον 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see at {γρ; ἅπαν ῥύπον} all of it **filth** (_accusative_), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.20}}; of a person, πρὸς τὸ μὴ λοῦσθαι ῥύπος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 10.4}}:—also **ῥύπος**, εος, τό, of cheese-**parings**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.64.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **sordidness, meanness**, ὁ ῥ. τοῦ χαμαὶ βίου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 7.47.}}
**II** **sealing-wax**, τοὺς ῥ. ἀνασπάσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1198.}}

4511:
** ῥύσις** [ῠ], εως, ἡ,
(ῥέω) **flow**, ὕδατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 944b}}; εἰς τὸ κοιλότατον ἡ ῥ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 355b17}}; opposed to ἄμπωτις,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 366a19; ἐκ ῥινῶν αἵματος -ιες 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.27; κοιλίης [prev. work] 2.14}}; [ἐλαίου], i.e. **yield** of oil, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.14.10, “PRev.Laws” 60.16 (3rd c.BC)}}; φλεβῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 668a11}}; of fire, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 54}}; **shedding**, τριχῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.120, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.431}}; used for γονόρροια, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.15.2f}}
**II** **course of a river, stream**, ποταμὸς ποιεῖται τὴν ῥ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.16.6, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.9, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.35, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.4.}}
**III** in Mathematics texts, [στιγμῆς] ῥύσιν φασὶν εἶναι οἱ γεωμέτραι τὴν γραμμήν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|compare (ῥυΐσκομαι, ῥέω 1.5b) 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “in Nicomachi arithmeticam introductionem” p.57 P., compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.7.3, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.97 F.}}; οἷον ἄστρου ῥ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 395b8.}}

4517:
** ῥώννῡμι**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τροφῆς” 2}}; **ῥωννύω**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 103e, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.581:}}_future_ ῥώσω (ἐπιρ-) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.9e}}: _aorist_ ἔρρωσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 335, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 76, (ἐπ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.14, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.36}}:—_passive_, ῥώννῠμαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 25, “Cor.” 24, etc.}}: _future_ ῥωσθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 1.6.3, (ἐπιρ-) 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Somn.” 18}}: _aorist_ ἐρρώσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.72, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238c, (ἐπ-) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 661:}}_perfect_ ἔρρωμαι (see. below):—**strengthen**, τροφὴ ῥώννυσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus [same place]}}; ῥ. τὰν ψυχὰν ποτ᾽ (πρὸς) ἀλκάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 103b; ὁρμάν [prev. work]e; τὰς πόλεις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 19: but}}
**II** mostly in _perfect_ _passive_ (with _present_ sense) ἔρρωμαι, and _pluperfect_ ἐρρώμην (as _imperfect_):—**have strength** or **might**, γέροντές ἐσμεν κοὐδαμῶς ἐρρώμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 636; ἐρρῶσθαι τὴν ψυχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.29; so ἐρρώσθη χρήμασιν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 23.}}
**II.2** **to be eager, enthusiastic**, glossed by προθυμεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 411; ἔρρωντο ἐς τὸν πόλεμον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8, compare 8.78, 4.72}}: with _infinitive_, **to be eager** to do, ἔρρωτο πᾶς ξυνεπιλαμβάνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.8, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 176b.}}
**II.3** **to be in good health**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.15: frequently in _imperative_ ἔρρωσο}}, **farewell**, the usual way of ending a letter, as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.33}}, and at the close of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 1, 2, and 10; ἔρρωσο πολλά 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 84; ἔρρωσθε [prev. author] “Pk.” 50; also φράζειν τινὶ ἐρρῶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.152, 19.248; εἰ ἔρρωσαι.. καλῶς ἂν ἔχοι, ἐρρώμεθα δὲ καὶ αὐτοί “PPetr.” 2p.27 (3rd c.BC)}}; εἰ ἔρρωσθε εὖ ἂν ἔχοι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 7.413 (Oropus, Senatus consultum, 2nd c.BC),compare “SIG” 768 (Epist. Aug.).}}
**II.4** frequently in _participle_ ἐρρωμένος (which see).

4521:
** Σάββᾰτ-ον**, τό,
the Hebrew {{Refs|“Sabbath”, i.e. “Rest”}} (δηλοῖ δὲ ἀνάπαυσιν.. τὸ ὄνομα {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.1.1), LXX.Exo.16.23, +others, NT.Mark.6.2, +others}}: frequently in _plural_ of the single day, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 762.6 (3rd c.BC), LXX.4Ki.4.23; ὀψὲ σαββάτων NT.Matt.28.1}}; ἡ ἡμέρα τῶν σ. {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Num.15.32, NT.Luke.4.16, +others (but ἡ ἡμ. τοῦ σ. [prev. work] 13.14): irregular _dative_ _plural_ σάββασι (ν) LXX.1Mac.2.38, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.7.3, +others, NT.Mark.2.23, +others}}, frequently with variant{σαββάτοις}; but σάββασι is certain in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.159 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** **period of seven days, week**, εἰς μίαν σαββάτων toward the first day **of the week,** {{NT|NT.Matt.28.1; κατὰ μίαν σαββάτου NT.1Cor.16.2; πρώτῃ σαββάτου NT.Mark.16.9}}; τῇ μιᾷ τῶν σ{{NT|. [prev. work] 2, NT.John.20.1}}; δὶς τοῦ σ. {{NT|NT.Luke.18.12.}}
**3** **Σαβάτ**, the {{LXX|11th month of the Hebrew year, nearly = February, LXX.1Mac.16.14.}}

4522:
** σᾰγήν-η**,
{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria}}**ἁγάνα** (see. ἄγανα and compare σάγανα), ἡ, **large drag-net** for taking fish, **seine**, Ital. **sagena**, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Hab.1.15, +others, NT.Matt.13.47, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.169c, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 22, “Pisc.” 51, etc.; σαγήνην βάλλειν 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 4.1, 9.6}}; **hunting-net**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 43.8.}}
**2** ={ἐπίπλοος} (c), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.169.}}

4525:
** σαίνω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 771}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ σαῖνον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.219}}: _aorist_ ἔσηνα {{Refs|17.302}}; _Doric dialect_ ἔσᾱνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.6, “P.” 1.52}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 194}}:—properly of dogs, **wag the tail, fawn**, ὅτ᾽ ἂν ἀμφὶ ἄνακτα κύνες.. σαίνωσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.217; νόησε δὲ δῖος Ὀδυσσεὺς σαίνοντάς τε κύνας 16.6; σαίνεις δάκνουσα καὶ κύων λαίθαργος εἶ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 885; ἡ κύων ἔσηνε καὶ προσῆλθ᾽ 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus Comedy texts 14.5: with the _dative_ added, οὐρῇ μέν ῥ᾽ ὅ γ᾽ ἔσηνε}}, of the dog Argus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.302; σ. οὐρῇ τε καὶ οὔασι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus [same place]; ἔσαινεν οὐρᾷ με 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 687 (ἔσαινεν οὐράν}} **wagged** his tail, Hemsterhuis, compare {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia recentia 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 704, Scholia recentia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.109).}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of persons, **fawn, cringe**, ὑδαρεῖ σ. φιλότητι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 798 (_anapaest meter_), compare “Pers.” 97 (Lyric poetry, assuming variant); also σ. ποτὶ πάντας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.82}}; σ. ποτὶ ἀγγελίαν greet it with joy, {{Refs|[prev. author] “O.” 4.6.}}
**III** with _accusative_ _person_, **fawn upon**, κέρκῳ τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1031, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.604 (3rd c.BC(?): Nossis Epigrammatica)}}; so of fishes, σαίνοντες οὐραίοισι τὴν κεκτημένην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 762.}}
**III.2** **fawn on, pay court to, greet**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.52; ὅτ᾽ ἐλεύθερος ἀτμένα σαίνει 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 1.1.19}}; σ. μόρον **cringe to** it, **seek to avert** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 383, 704}}; παιδός με σαίνει φθόγγος **greets** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1214}}; φαιδρὰ γοῦν ἀπ᾽ ὀμμάτων σαίνει με **greets** me gladly from her eyes, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 320.}}
**III.3** **gladden**, especially with hope or conviction, ἐλπίδι κέαρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.55; οὐ γάρ με σ. θέσφατα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 685 (Lyric poetry); τὰ λεγόμενα.. σ. τὴν ψυχήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1090a37}}; so σ. τὴν ὑπόσχεσιν **receive** it **with marks of gladness**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 20 (uncertain, σαίνει τῇ ὑποσχέσει is probably conjecture)}}:—_passive_, σαίνομαι δ᾽ ὑπ᾽ ἐλπίδος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 194.}}
**III.4** **beguile, cozen, deceive**, ἡ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἐν σκότῳ λήθουσά με ἔσαιν᾽ Ἐρινύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 577}}; σ. μ᾽ ἔννυχος φρυκτωρία **seeks to deceive** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 55 (or in signification 111.3).}}
**III.5** in {{NT|NT.1Thes.3.3, σαίνεσθαι ἐν ταῖς θλίψεσι}} seems to mean **to be shaken, disturbed**; σαινόμενοι τοῖς λεγομένοις ἐδάκρυον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.41 (or in signification 111.4)}}; σαίνεται· κινεῖται, σαλεύεται, ταράττεται, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; but compare σιαίνω.

4526:
** σάκκος**
or **σάκος**, ὁ, see entry near the end:— **coarse cloth of hair**, especially of goats' hair, σάκκος τρίχινος {{LXX+NT|NT.Rev.6.12, cf. LXX.Isa.50.3, LXX.Sir.25.17.}}
**II** **anything made of this cloth**:
**II.1** **sack, bag**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.80, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 745, “Lys.” 1209, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.559,8.672:—as a measure, “Ostr.” 1096, +others}}
**II.2** **sieve, strainer**, especially for wine, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 57, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.19; σ. τρίχινοι “PHamb.” 10.39 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.3** **coarse garment, sackcloth**, worn as mourning by the Jews, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Gen.37.34, NT.Luke.10.13, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.12.5, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.239c.}}
**III** **coarse beard**, like rough hair-cloth, σάκον πρὸς ταῖν γνάθοιν ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 502; compare σακεσφόρος 11.}} —The form σάκος is said to be _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 296, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 229, 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus p.354 P., 13th-14th c.AD: Thomas Magister Grammaticus p.344}} R., etc.; while σάκκος is called _Doric dialect_ by {{Refs 6th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista [same place], Hellenic by 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus and 13th-14th c.AD: Thomas Magister Grammaticus [same places], Comic by 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.191. In 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 822, “Ec.” 502, σάκος is required by the metre, as is σάκκος in “Ach.” 745 (from Megarian), and in 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus [same place]; codices of 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus give σάκκος}}. Inscrr. have σάκος {{Refs|“IG” 22.1672.73,74, 108 and σάκκος [prev. work] 198}}: Papyri have σάκος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 753.27 (3rd c.BC), “UPZ” 84.52 (2nd c.BC)}}, but oftener σάκκος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.427.1, 14 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 116.3 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc. (Prob. the word, like the thing, was borrowed from Phoenicia, cf. Hebrew **saq.**)

4529:
** Σᾰλᾰμίς**
(also in Grammars **Σᾰλᾰμίν**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, Eust.ad 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 498)}}, _genitive_ ῖνος, ἡ, “Salamis”, an island and town of the same name, between Athens and Megara, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.557, etc.}}
**II** a town of Cyprus founded by Teucer of Salamis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Hymni Homerici” 10.4, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.162, etc.}}; **Σαλαμίνη**, [{{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {Ἐπιφάνιος}.}}]

4531:
** σαλ-εύω**,
_future_ σαλεύσω {{LXX|LXX.Wis.4.19}}: _aorist_ ἐσάλευσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.95, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.83}}:— _passive_, _future_ σαλευθήσομαι {{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.16.18, NT.Luke.21.26}}: _aorist_ ἐσαλεύθην {{LXX+NT+5th c.BC+|LXX.1Mac.9.13, NT.Act.4.31, NT.2Thes.2.2, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator [same place]:}}_perfect_ σεσάλευμαι (see. below): (σάλος):—**cause to rock, make to vibrate** or **oscillate**, with _accusative_, [τὰς ἀγκύρας] οὐδεὶς χειμὼν σαλεύει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.29}}; σ. τρικυμίᾳ πέδον, of the sea, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 475}}; of an earthquake, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.83 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), compare 259 ([prev. author]): _metaphorically_, δόξαν σ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1123f, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.56, 337, etc.; σ. τινὰ ἐκ θεμελίων LXX.Wis.4.19; ἐπιστολαὶ δυνάμεναι λίθον σαλεῦσαι}}**heartrending,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 528.12 (2nd c.AD)}}; σ. τοὺς ὄχλους **stir** them **up,** {{LXX+NT|NT.Acts.17.13, cf. LXX.Sir.28.14}}:—_passive_, **to be shaken to and fro, waver, totter, reel**, χθὼν σεσάλευται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1081; κύκλος σαλευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 79e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 857a7, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lassitudine” 11}}; of teeth or nails, **to be loosened**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.871, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.3}}; of persons, ἐκ Βρομίου γυῖα σαλευόμενον {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.26 (6th c.AD: Marcus Argentarius Epigrammaticus), compare 12.31 (Phanias Epigrammaticus)}}; ὑφ᾽ ἡδονῆς σαλευομένη κορώνη {{Refs 7th c.BC+|Scholia 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1009 (wrongly attributed to 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus, “fragment” 102)}}; later simply, **stir, move**, κατεσχέθην νόσῳ.. ὡς μὴ δύνασθαι μηδὲ σαλεύεσθαι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PSI” 4.299.4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** **shake in measuring**, so as to give good measure, μέτρον σεσαλευμένον {{NT|NT.Luke.6.38; compare σαλάσσω 11.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **move up and down, roll, toss**, especially of ships in a stormy sea or persons in them, σ. ἐν πλοίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.17, compare 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.4, etc.}}: generally, **put out to sea**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 77}}: _metaphorically_, **toss like a ship at sea, to be tempest-tossed, be in sore distress**, πόλις γὰρ.. ἄγαν ἤδη σαλεύει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 23; πρόδοτος δὲ.. σ. Ἠλέκτρα [prev. author] “El.” 1074 (Lyric poetry); ὅταν.. σαλεύῃ πόλις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 249 (Lyric poetry), compare “OGI” 515.47 (Mylasa, 3rd c.AD)}}; ἐν νόσοις ἢ γήρᾳ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 923b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 883a34}}; ἐν κινδύνῳ σ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.11; σ. ὑπὲρ ἑαυτοῦ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 48}}; **to be unstable**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.121}}; **flicker**, of the eye-balls in nystagmus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).68}}; **oscillate**, of the λόγος ἐνδιάθετος, ἐν τούτοις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.65.}}
**II.2** of ships also, ἐπ᾽ ἀγκυρῶν **ride** at anchor, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 2.2.7: _metaphorically_, ὡς ἐπ᾽ ἀγκύρας τῆς φύσεως σ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.493d; σ. ἐπὶ τῶν ἐλπίδων 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 1.26}}; also ὁρῶν ἡμᾶς ἐπὶ τούτῳ μόνῳ (i.e. τῷ υἱῷ) σαλεύοντας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 38}}; γραῦν ἐπὶ ἑνὶ γομφίῳ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.28, compare “POxy.” 472.50 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐπὶ τοιούτοις παραγγέλμασιν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 2.12 (hence later in a causal sense, σ. ἐπί τινι τὰς ἐλπίδας}} **anchor** them upon.., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.33).}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **roll like a ship, roll in one's walk**, of persons with the hip-joints far apart, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 56.}}

4535:
** σάλος** [ᾰ], ὁ,
**tossing motion**, of an earthquake, χθονὸς νῶτα σεισθῆναι σάλῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 46}}; especially **rolling swell** of the sea, πόντου σ., πόντιος σ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 28, “IT” 1443: _plural_, πόντιοι σ. [prev. author] “Or.” 994 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **open roadstead, roads**, opposed to a harbour, ἐν σάλῳ στῆναι, ={σαλεύειν}{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|11.2; ἀλίμενον μὲν σάλους δὲ ἔχον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.53.10; οὔτε λιμὴν οὔτε σ. ἐπ᾽ ἀγκύρας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.44, compare 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 92, “Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 7 (_plural_), 55.}}
**II** of ships or persons in them, **tossing on the sea**, ἐκ πολλοῦ σ. εὕδοντ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἀκτῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 271; σάλον εἶχεν ἡ θάλασσα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 10}}; καρηβαρεῖν ὑπὸ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 28; ἐν τοσούτῳ σ. ναυτιάσαντα [prev. author] “Tox.” 19}}: _metaphorically_ of the ship of the state, τὰ μὲν δὴ πόλεος θεοὶ πολλῷ σ. σείσαντες ὤρθωσαν πάλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 163; πόλις.. σαλεύει κἀνακουφίσαι κάρα βυθῶν ἔτ᾽ οὐχ οἵα τε φοινίον σάλου [prev. author] “OT” 24; ἐν σάλῳ πόλις γενομένη 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.49}}; ἔσχε.. ὁ ἀγὼν ὑποτροπὴν καὶ σ. began to **waver**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 32, compare “Aem.” 18; compare σαλεύω 11.1.}}
**II.2** **distemper, restlessness, perplexity**, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Sir.40.5, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.816; τῆς ψυχῆς 2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 1.1.}}

4536:
** σάλπ-ιγξ**, ιγγος, ἡ,
**war-trumpet**, ὅτε τ᾽ ἴαχε σάλπιγξ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.219; σ. ἡ ἱερά 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.56, compare 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Mensibus” 4.73:—on various σάλπιγγες, see 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.85f, Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place]:— σ. Τυρσηνική 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 568, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1378, “Heracl.” 831}}; ὑπαὶ σάλπιγγος by sound **of trumpet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 711, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1001}}; also ἀπὸ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 3.12, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.13.1.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, Πιερικὰ σ., of Pindar, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.34 (Antip. Sid.)}}; οὐρανίη σ. **thunder**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 327, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 2.558.}}
**II** ={σάλπισμα}, cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408a9.}}
**III** σ. θαλασσία, ={στρόμβος}{{Refs 7th c.BC+|2, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 192}}; compare σάλπη.
**IV** name of a **bird**, [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (perhaps ={σαλπιγκτής} 2).}}]
**V** a kind of comet, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 90.}}
**VI** epithet of Athena at Argos, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 915,986, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.21.3.}}

4537:
** σαλπ-ίζω**,
_future_ **-ιῶ**{{LXX|LXX.Num.10.4}}: _aorist_ ἐσάλπιγξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 19}}; _Epic dialect_ σάλπιγξα (see. below):—later, _future_ σαλπίσω {{NT|NT.1Cor.15.52}}: _aorist_ ἐσάλπισα {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Josh.6.13, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ocyp.” 114, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.130b, etc.:—}}_passive_, _perfect_ σεσάλπιγκται (περι-) Eudamidas cited in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.13.65}}:—**σαλπίσσω** is Tarentine, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1654.24, “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.62}}; **σαλπίττω** _Attic dialect_, cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Jud.Voc.” 10, variant in 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.86}}; **σαλπίδδω** _Boeotian dialect_, {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 4.325}}:—**sound the trumpet**, σάλπιγξι ῥυθμοὺς σ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.32: with _accusative_ _cognate_, σ. πολέμου κτύπον Batrachomyomachia 200; σ. ἀνακλητικόν “Anthologia Graeca” 11.136 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus); λιγὺν ἦχον 3rd c.BC: Hedylus Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.497d; τὸ.. δείπνου σημεῖον 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.130b: _absolutely_, ὅταν ποιῇς ἐλεημοσύνην, μὴ σαλπίσῃς ἔμπροσθέν σου NT.Matt.6.2: _metaphorically_, ἀμφὶ δὲ σάλπιγξεν μέγας οὐρανός}} heaven **trumpeted** around, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.388: _impersonal_, ἐπεὶ ἐσάλπιγξε (i.e. ὁ σαλπιγκτής)}} when **the trumpet sounded**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.17, cf. NT.1Cor.15.52.}}
**2** with _accusative_, ἡμέραν σ. **proclaim, announce** day, of the cock, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ocyp.” 114.}}

4538:
Included with: ** σαλπ-ιγκτής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**trumpeter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.69, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.29, 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.9, 6.3, etc.; this spelling has the best M 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}} authority and occurs in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.299 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}, but has not been found in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr.; **σαλπικτής**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 153.68 (Delos, 4th c.BC), “IG” 3.1288, “POxy.” 519.16 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **σαλπικτάς** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 7.1773 (Thespiae, 2nd c.AD)}}, **σαλπιγκτάς** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work]3195 (Orchom., 1st c.BC)}}; later **σαλπιστής**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|[prev. work]9(2).525.4 (Thessaly, 2nd c.BC), 3.1285 (Attica, 1st c.AD), 7.3196 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_), “Sammelb.” 4591.3, etc.; also in 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.45.13, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.17, NT.Rev.18.22, etc.}}
**2** ={ὄρνις ὁμοίως σάλπιγγι φθεγγόμενος}, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}; ={ὀρχίλος}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

4547:
** σανδάλ-ιον** [ᾰ], τό,
_diminutive_ of σάνδαλον, mostly in _plural_, **sandals**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91 (_sing._), 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 131, 5th-6th c.BC: Cephisodorus Comicus 4, LXX.Josh.9.5.}}
**2** **horseshoe**, σ. ὀνικά {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 741.10 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** a surgical bandage, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus (?)cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 49.35.3}}, as variant for **σανδάλιος**, ὁ, which is found also in Heraclas cited in [same work]{{Refs|48.4.}}
**III** see at {σάνδαλον} {{Refs|11.}}

4548:
** σᾰνίς**, ίδος, ἡ,
**board, plank, timber**, σ. πτελεΐνη {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12.313.133, compare 22.1672.168, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.22.9, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.269 (Antip. Thess.), NT.Act.27.44, etc.; σ. ἄξοος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 105}}:—hence **anything made thereof**,
**1** **door**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always in _plural_, **folding doors**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.453, 461, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.128, etc.}}; κολληταὶ σ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.583; σ. πυκινῶς ἀραρυῖαι, δικλίδες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.344, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.535; πύλῃσιν ἐπικεκλιμέναι σ. 12.121}}: rarely in _singular_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1221.}}
**2** **wooden platform, scaffold**, or **stage**, ἐφ᾽ ὑψηλῆς σ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.51.}}
**3** **wooden floor**, especially **ship's deck**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1556, 3rd c.BC: Archimelus Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.209c, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 48.}}
**4** **bench, seat**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 244 B 61 (Delph., 4th c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.5.}}
**5** **lid** of box, variant in {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.12.9.}}
**6** in _plural_, **wooden tablets** for writing on, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 967 (Lyric poetry)}}: especiallyat Athens and elsewhere, **tablets covered with gypsum**, on which were written all sorts of public notices, especially the causes for hearing in the law-courts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 349, 848}}; laws to be proposed, Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.84}}; laws corrected by the Thesmothetae, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.39}}; lists of officers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.10; accounts, “IG” 12.374.190}}; names of debtors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.70 (in _sing._), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.237: _sing._ also in “SIG” 975.30 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}; at Rome, of **the tables** on which the laws were written, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.32.}}
**6.b** _plural_, **painted panels, pictures**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 977a10 (Delos, 2nd c.BC).}}
**7** **plank to which offenders were bound** or **nailed**, ζῶντα πρὸς σανίδα διε- (variant{προσδιε-}) πασσάλευσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.33; σανίδι προσπασσαλεύσαντες [prev. author] 9.120, compare 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 341; ἐν τῇ σ. δῆσαι, πρὸς τῇ σ. δεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 931, 940; σανίσι προσδῆσαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Duris Epigrammaticus 67 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}

4550:
** σαπρός**, ά, όν,
(σήπω) **rotten, putrid**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 23, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὀστέων φύσιος” 13}}; of the lungs, **diseased**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Morb.” 1.13}}; of bone, **carious**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Fract.” 33}}; of wood, etc., **rotten**, ἱστίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 918; βύρσα [prev. author] “V.” 38}}; πινακίσκος, φορμός, σχοινίον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 813, 542, “V.” 1343; ἱμάς 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 109.4}}; τοῦ διατειχίσματος ἀνελόντι τὰ σ. {{Refs|“IG” 22.1672.24}}; of a house, σ. καὶ ῥέουσα καὶ καταπίπτουσα Telesp.27 H.; ἐλαῖαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.14.10: proverbial, σαπροῦ πείσματος ἀντιλαβέσθαι 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1362}}: especially, of fish that have been long in pickle, **stale, rancid**, τάριχος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1101}}; opposed to πρόσφατος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 218.4, compare 125.6}}; of withered flowers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.70.}} _adverb_, -ρῶς λούει τὰ βαλανεῖα **so as to leave** one **filthy**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.21.14.}}
**II** generally, **stale, worn out**, ἀρχαῖον καὶ σαπρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 323}}; of clothes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 26.6 (2nd c.AD).}} _adverb_, -ῶς (perhaps misspelt for -ός) περιπατῶ I am walking about **in rags**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 846.9 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** of persons, γέρων ὢν καὶ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 698}}; ὦ σαπρά, to an old woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ec.” 884, 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 10; so εἶναι σαπρὸν κοὐδὲν δύνασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1380}}; οὐδέν ἐσμεν οἱ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 221; σ. γυναῖκα.. ὁ τρόπος εὔμορφον ποιεῖ 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 170.}}
**II.3** of wine, **mellow** (compare σαπρίας), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 442, compare 5th c.BC: Philyllius Comicus 24; τρὺξ παλαιὰ καὶ σαπρά 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1086}}; of old wine, ὀδόντας οὐκ ἔχων, ἤδη σαπρὸς.., γέρων γε δαιμονίως {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 167.4.}}
**II.4** εἰρήνη σαπρά, a joke παρὰ προσδοκίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 554.}}
**II.5** _metaphorically_, **unsound, bad**, λόγος {{NT|NT.Eph.4.29}}; opposed to καλός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 36.30, compare “PSI” 4.312.13 (4th c.AD)}}; ἄρουραι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 13.22 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὰ σ. ταῦτα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.16.7; ὡς σ. καὶ κίβδηλος ὁ λέγων.. 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 11.15, compare “Sammelb.” 5761.23 (1st c.AD), “PSI” 6.717.4 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὴν σ. εἱμαρμένην the **evil** fate, {{Refs|“PMag.Leid.W.” 14.38.}}
**III** of sound, αὐλεῖ γὰρ σαπρὰ.. κρουμάτια [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 50 (perhaps _falsa lectio_ for{σαθρά}, see at {σαθρός} 2).}}]

4553:
** σαργάν-η** [γᾰ], ἡ,
= ταργάνη, **plait, braid**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 788 codices (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **basket**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 29.6, 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 21.7, NT.2Cor.11.33, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 6, “PFlor.” 269.7 (3rd c.AD), “PLond.” 2.236.11 (4th c.AD):—see. σάρκινος 111.}}

4559:
** σάρκ-ῐνος**, η, ον,
**of** or **like flesh, fleshy**, σ. ὄζος (see. ὄζος) σ. [μέρη] **fleshy** parts, such as the gums, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 493a1}}; **made of flesh** (and blood), {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1117b5}}; ἄνθρωποι θνατοὶ καὶ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.44.81, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 6, “Sign.” 34}}; σ. ἰχθῦς (opposed to a dream) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.66}}; τοῖς τὸ χρήσιμον καὶ σ. καὶ ὠφέλιμον [ἔχουσι τῶν λόγων] **substantial**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.79c.}}
**2** **made of gut**, σχοινία {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.1177.169 (2nd c.AD).}}
**3** **fleshly, of the flesh, Ep. Hebrew **{{NT|7.16, variant in NT.Rom.7.14.}}
**II** **fleshy, corpulent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 711, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 387; σώματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 906c.}}
**III** σάρκινος ἤτοι γυργαθός, perhaps ={σαργάνη}{{Refs|2, “Edict.Diocl.” 32.18.}}

4560:
** σάρκ-ῐνος**, η, ον,
**of** or **like flesh, fleshy**, σ. ὄζος (see. ὄζος) σ. [μέρη] **fleshy** parts, such as the gums, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 493a1}}; **made of flesh** (and blood), {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1117b5}}; ἄνθρωποι θνατοὶ καὶ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.44.81, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 6, “Sign.” 34}}; σ. ἰχθῦς (opposed to a dream) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.66}}; τοῖς τὸ χρήσιμον καὶ σ. καὶ ὠφέλιμον [ἔχουσι τῶν λόγων] **substantial**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.79c.}}
**2** **made of gut**, σχοινία {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.1177.169 (2nd c.AD).}}
**3** **fleshly, of the flesh, Ep. Hebrew **{{NT|7.16, variant in NT.Rom.7.14.}}
**II** **fleshy, corpulent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 711, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 387; σώματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 906c.}}
**III** σάρκινος ἤτοι γυργαθός, perhaps ={σαργάνη}{{Refs|2, “Edict.Diocl.” 32.18.}}

4561:
** σάρξ**,
_genitive_ σαρκός, ἡ, _Aeolic dialect_ **σύρξ** {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 708.31}}:— **flesh**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always in _plural_, except {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.450, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 364, 461; κορέει κύνας.. δημῷ καὶ σάρκεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.380; ἔγκατά τε σάρκας τε καὶ ὀστέα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.293, compare 11.219; σάρκες περιτρομέοντο μέλεσσιν 18.77, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 538, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 168, etc.; τούτου σάρκας λύκοι πάσονται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1040; ὀπτὰς σάρκας [prev. author] “Ag.” 1097; σάρκες δ᾽ ἀπ᾽ ὀστέων.. ἀπέρρεον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1200}}; sometimes to represent the whole body, μήτε γῆ δέξαιτό μου σάρκας θανόντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 1031, compare 1239, 1343 (_anapaest meter_): _sing._ later in same sense, τοῦ αἵματος.. πηγνυμένου σ. γίνεται (of the foetus) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 15, compare “Steril.” 233; κορέσαι στόμα πρὸς χάριν ἐμᾶς σαρκὸς αἰόλας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1157 (Lyric poetry); ἔδαπτον σάρκα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1189, compare “Ba.” 1136, “Cyc.” 344, etc.}}: also collectively, of the **body**, γέροντα τὸν νοῦν, σάρκα δ᾽ ἡβῶσαν φέρει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 622; σαρκὶ παλαιᾶ [prev. author] “Ag.” 72 (_anapaest meter_)}}; σαρκὸς περιβόλαια, ἐνδυτά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1269, “Ba.” 746:— 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}} uses _singular_ and _plural_ in much the same manner, ταῖς σαρξὶ σάρκες προσγένωνται {{Refs|“Phd.” 96d, compare “Smp.” 211e, “R.” 556d, “Grg.” 518c, etc.; τῆς σαρκὸς διαλυτικόν “Ti.” 60b, compare 61c, 62b}}, etc.: **portions of meat**, usually in _plural_, σάρκας τρεῖς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(7).237.17 (from Amorgos) (_sing._, [prev. work]12(2).498.16 (Methymna, 3rd c.BC))}}; but, **pieces of flesh** or **membrane**, βήσσοντα.. ὥστε σάρκας ἐνπύους.. ἀποβάλλειν {{Refs|“SIG” 1171.5 (from Lebena).}}
**b** εἰς σάρκα πημαίνειν to the **quick**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1289p.60V.}}
**2** ἡ σ. τοῦ σκύτεος the **inner** or **flesh-side** of leather, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 33.}}
**3** **fleshy, pulpy substance of fruit**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.8.5, “HP” 1.2.6, 4.15.1, +others}}
**II** the **flesh**, as the seat of the affections and lusts, **fleshly nature**, ἐν τῇ σ. ἡ ἡδονή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 18, compare “Sent.Vat.” 33}}; ἀδούλωτον (probable reading) τῇ σαρκὶ καὶ τοῖς ταύτης πάθεσι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.107f}}, compare 101b; frequently in **NT,** {{NT|NT.Gal.5.19, +others}}
**II.2** in “NT” also, the **body**, τῆς σαρκὸς πρόνοια {{NT|NT.Rom.13.14; οὔτε ἡ σ. αὐτοῦ εἶδεν διαφθοράν NT.Act.2.31, etc.: hence (partly as a Hebraism) πᾶσα σάρξ}}, = every-**body**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.6.12, +others, NT.Luke.3.6, etc.}}; οὐ.. πᾶσα σάρξ no**body, **{{NT|NT.Matt.24.22, etc.}}
**II.3** the **physical** or **natural order** of things, opposed to the spiritual or supernatural, σοφοὶ κατὰ σάρκα {{NT|NT.1Cor.1.26; ἐν Χριστῷ Ἰησοῦ καὶ οὐκ ἐν σαρκὶ πεποιθότες NT.Phil.3.3}}; τὸν κύριον τῶν πνευμάτων καὶ πάσης σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 1181.3 (2nd c.BC, Jewish). (Perh. I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **twr[kcirc]-** 'portion', cf. Avest. θ**war[schwa]s-** 'cut'.)

4563:
** σᾰρόω**,
= σαίρω (B), **sweep clean**, τὴν οἰκίαν {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.15.8, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.33, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 253.7}}:—_passive_, οἶκος σεσαρωμένος {{NT|NT.Matt.12.44, etc.}}: _metaphorically_, **to be swept clean, exhausted,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PGiss. 11.19 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** _passive_, of the thing **swept**, κῦμα.. μεταξὺ χοιράδων σαρούμενον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 389. Rejected by 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 63.}}

4570:
** σβέννῡμι**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.66, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 835e, etc.}}; **σβεννύω**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.5, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 54, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 19, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ἐσβέννυον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.21.4:}}_future_ σβέσω App {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“BC” 2.68, (κατα-) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 958, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 633}}; _Epic dialect_ σβέσσω Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.77, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.26}}: _aorist_ ἔσβεσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.293 (tmesis), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1057, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 778 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ σβέσσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.621}}; _Ionic dialect_ _infinitive_ κατα-ς βῶσαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.39:}}_perfect_ and _aorist_ 2, see below:—_middle_, _future_ σβήσομαι (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 805c}}: _aorist_ σβέσαντο {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.795}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 590}}: _future_ σβεσθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.17}}: _aorist_ ἐσβέσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”) , (κατ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.8}}; _Epic dialect_ συν-έσβετο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 2.477, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἔσβεσμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 21.1, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 9.54, etc., (ἀπ-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 43}}:—besides these, _aorist 2_ and _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ _active_ are used _intransitive_, ἔσβην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.471, (ἀπ-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 971, (κατ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.5}}; _imperative_ σβῆτε (_transitive_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus in “Stud.Ital.” 10.123}}; _participle_ ἀπο-σβείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.31:}}_perfect_ ἔσβηκα (ἀπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.13, (κατ-) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 888:}}_pluperfect_ ἐσβήκει (ἀπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 218b}}:—**quench, put out**, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in the literal sense only in _compound_ κατα-σβέννυμι (which see)}}; σ. τὸ καιόμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.66; κεραυνόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.6; φλόγα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.668.}}
**2** of liquids, **dry up**, ἡ Μηδικὴ πόα σ. τὸ γάλα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 522b25, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 9.549 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus).}}
**3** generally and _metaphorically_, **quench, quell, check**, κεῖνός γ᾽ οὐκ ἐθέλει σβέσσαι χόλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.678; ἀνθρώπων σβέσσαι μένος 16.621; ὕβριν 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus (132) cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.77, cf. Oracle texts cited in [same work] 8.77, 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 43, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 835e; εἰ μὴ θεῶν τις τήνδε πεῖραν ἔσβεσεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1057; ὡς φόνῳ σβέσῃ φόνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 40; ἔσβεσε κύματα νήνεμος αἴθρη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 778; σ. αὔξην καὶ ἐπιρροήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 783b; τὸν θυμόν [prev. work] 888a}}; καῦμα (in the bowels) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 54; ὁ βορέας σ. τὴν θερμότητα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 347b4}}; λιθάργυρον ὄξει ἢ οἴνῳ σ. **cooling** it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.87}}; ὕδατι δίψαν σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1349}}; σ. τυραννίδα epigram cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 20; κλέος “Anthologia Graeca” 9.104 (1st c.AD: Alpheus Epigrammaticus); Ἑλλάδα φωνήν [prev. work] 451}}; **regard as extinguishable**, ταύτας τὰς δυνάμεις {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.4.10.}}
**II** _passive_ σβέννυμαι (with _intransitive_ tenses of _active_, see above), **to be quenched, go out**, of fire, οὐδέ ποτ᾽ ἔσβη πῦρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.471}}; of inflamed pustules, **go down, disappear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”)}}; ἰχθύων.. ᾠὰ μετὰ ἁλῶν σβεσθέντα (assuming variant) καὶ ἐποπτηθέντα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.121c}}: _metaphorically_ of men, **become extinct, die,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 7.20 (4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus ?)}}; of a city,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 9.178 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** of liquids, **run dry**, γάλα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 587b28; πηγαί “Anthologia Graeca” 9.128; αἷμα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.49d}}; αἶγες σβεννύμεναι goats **which are going off their milk**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 590.}}
**II.3** generally, **to be quelled** or **lulled**, of wind, οὐδέ ποτ᾽ ἔσβη οὖρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.182}}; of sound, σβέννυτο θωρήκων ἐνοπή {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 10: _metaphorically_, τὸ μάχιμον σβεννύμενον ὑπὸ γήρως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 8}}; ἐσβέσθη Νίκανδρος his charm **is quenched,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 12.39}}; of an orator, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 4; ἐσβέσθη τὰ φίλτρα “Anthologia Graeca” 7.221, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.33, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 21.1}}; of legal proceedings, **to be cancelled**, διὰ τὸ ἐσβέσθαι πᾶν σπέρμα δίκης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“PMonac.” 1.43 (6th c.AD). (I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **zg[uglide]es-**, compare ζείναμεν· σβέννυμεν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}, Lithuanian _gèsti_ 'to be extinguished'.)

4571:
** σύ** [ῠ],
**thou**: _pronoun_ of the second _person_:—_Epic dialect_ _nominative_ **τύνη** [ῡ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.485, +others}} (_Laconian dialect_ **τούνη** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **σύ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 16.6, 21.9}}; _Doric dialect_ **τύ** [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.85, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 34, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.33, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **τού** [short syllable] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.83, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}} (also τούν {{Refs|[prev. work] 50.27, 55.6): Nom.}}**σύ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.31, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1035, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 29, etc.; _vocative_, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.193, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 165, “Pl.” 1069.}}—Gen. **σοῦ**, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 29.4}}, elsewhere only _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 302, etc.}}; _enclitic_ σου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 761, “OT” 538, etc.; never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses σεῦ, lines 3.206, +others, σέο [prev. work] 446, +others}} (also in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus (?)in “PLit.Lond.” 54, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.65), σεῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.137, +others; also σέθεν 1.180, +others}} (which also occurs in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 33, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.9, and Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 264, +others)}}, and as _enclitic_ σευ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.811, +others, σεο 1.396: 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has only σέο 1.124}}, σεο (_enclitic_) {{Refs|[prev. work] 9, σεῦ [prev. passage], 3.42, 85, 7.38}} σευ (_enclitic_) {{Refs|3.36, 134, 7.49}}:—_Doric dialect_ τεῦ, τευ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.19, 10.36, etc.}}; rarely τέο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 17; lengthened τεοῦ 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 145, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 84, and τεοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.37, 468}}; _Boeotian dialect_ τεῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 24}}; _Doric dialect_ τεοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59; also τιοῦς 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.27; τεῦς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.126}}; τοι variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.25}}; _enclitic_ τεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 83; Cretan dialect τέορ 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; other _Doric dialect_ forms are τίω, τίως, both {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13, τίος 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 75.24.}}—Dat. **σοί**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.158, 167, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 88, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 8, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 7,99, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 6.86.ά, 7.52}}, etc.; _Doric dialect_ τοί {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 86 (oxytone)}}; _Doric dialect_, Lesb., and _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ τοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Alem. 33, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Frr.” 2.2,8, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 79, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 20, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 44, 75.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.76, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.104, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115, 3.35, 63, 85; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, Lesbian Lyric poetry, and _Ionic dialect_ Lyric poetry and Prose τοι is always _enclitic_, σοί never _enclitic_ (τοί and σοι are not found except σοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.359, 11.381, ς (οι) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170}}, and in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.270, 9.55}}; rarer than τοι in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 3.69, +others)}}; in _Attic dialect_ both σοί and σοι (_enclitic_) are used (σοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 361, etc., σοι [prev. work] 87, etc.)}}, τοί and τοι are not used; σοι is never elided except in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170, τοι is elided in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.60, 347, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55, [prev. author] “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15 ii 9, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28.2}}; _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also **τεΐν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.201, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.619, epigram cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.60, 61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 930}}; also **τίν** [ῐ], {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 16, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.29, 8.68 (uncertain reading)}}; **τίν** [ῑ], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” 6(5).4, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.20 (τίν}} before a consonant, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93); also τεΐ 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 53; τίνη 3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13.}}—Acc. **σέ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.256, +others}}; _enclitic_ σε,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|1.26, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.2, “Supp.” 23.9, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, etc.}}; in late Gr. σέν, {{Refs|“Anatolian Studies” p.76}}; _Doric dialect_ τέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 52, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.48, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.5, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 114}}; τ᾽ variant (codex R) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 779 (on the accent see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 54.14, 83.4)}}; τρέ (to be read τϝέ) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; or (_enclitic_) τυ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.69 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1225, “Ach.” 730 (uncertain in “Ach.” 779), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.56, 78, etc.:—also τίν 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 4, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 7.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.39, 55, 68.}}
**2** in combination with γε, **σύ γε,** **σέ γε**, etc. (compare ἔγωγε), **thou at least, for thy part**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and _Attic dialect_; _Doric dialect_ τύγε [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [272], 5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 1 (variant{τύ γα}); τύγᾰ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.69, 71}}]; _Boeotian dialect_ **τούγα** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}}: _dative_ **σοί γε** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.557}}: _accusative_ **σέ γε** {{Refs|10.96}}, etc.:—also **σύ περ** {{Refs|24.398.}}
**3** σύ with _infinitive_ (as _imperative_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 4.163.}}
**II** Dual _nominative_ and _accusative_ **σφῶϊ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.336, 4.286, +others}}, **you two, both of you**; **σφώ** (not σφῴ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 85.17), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.574, 11.782, 13.47, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 344, 1543, etc.}}—Gen. and Dat. **σφῶϊν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341, +others}}; _contraction_ σφῷν once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.62, and always in _Attic dialect_ (Hdn.Gr. 1.475) and Trag., e.g. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 12, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 342, “OT” 1495, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 892e (codices 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus have σφῶϊν in “Tht.” 193c, +others, σφῶν “Euthd.” 273e, +others)}}. None of these forms are _enclitic_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.9, 85.12; codex A of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 658c, 673e, 689a wrongly makes σφῳν}} _enclitic_; Ζεὺς σφὼ is prescribed in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.146 by Hdn.Gr. 2.93.}} —σφῶϊ is never _dative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.286}} it is the _accusative_ depending on κελεύ; σφῶϊν is never _accusative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.52 it is dative commodi.}}
**III** Plur. _nominative_ **ὑμεῖς**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.75, +others (before a vowel, 4.246, 7.194, +others), 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 11, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 29a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, etc.}}, **ye, you**; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.274, +others, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 24, 25}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 60, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 760, 761, 862; ὑμέν “SIG” 685.118 (Crete, 2nd c.BC), “GDI” 5155.6 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμές {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 6}}; a resolved form ὑμέες, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus “fragment” 14, is a poeticlicence (so 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.3)}} rather than genuine Ionic {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22).}}— Gen. **ὑμῶν**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 143, etc.}}; ὑμέων (_disyllable_) [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.159, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.7, +others, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.6 (ὑμῶν codices), 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.5 (variant{ὑμῶν})}}]; ὑμέων also {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73, 6.130, +others; as _trisyllable_, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.27; ὑμείων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.348, 7.195, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 46}}; also ὑμῶν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.23}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ὑμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 96}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμίων {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 22.}}—Dat. **ὑμῖν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ ὗμῐν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 97.28}} also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 91}}; _Doric dialect_ (not _enclitic_) ὑμίν [ῐ] {{Refs|[prev. author] 92}}; ὑμίν [ῐ] also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 864, 1242, “OT” 991, 1402, “Ant.” 308, “El.” 804, +others (but ὗμιν}} should perhaps be _restored_ where the sense needs an enclitic on the principle stated by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 35.6, 36.5, “Synt.” 130.23)}}; ὕμιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(as _enclitic_) is prescribed by Hdn.Gr. (2.124) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.33, by “Etymologicum Magnum” 432.34 in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.376, 2.141, etc.:—_Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμῐ, ὔμμῐν, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, 11.336, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 328, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 14, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.9, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).17.}}—Acc. **ὑμᾶς**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325}}, etc. (**-υ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 820, variant in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 222}}; ὗμας or (more probably) ὕμας is required by the metre in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 9.9, 47.11)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ὑμέας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.198, +others}}; _enclitic_ ὕμεας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.60 (Papyrus)}}; ὑμέας also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, +others}}; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.412, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).19; also in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 620 (trimeter), and 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 846 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 3, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 737, “Lys.” 1076, “SIG” 528.3 (Cretan dialogue, 3rd c.BC), 622 B 8 (Cretan, 2nd c.BC).}}—The _plural_ is sometimes used in addressing **one** person, when others are included in the speaker's thought, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.81, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 89. (With σύ}} cf. Latin _tu_, Gothic _pu_; with τοι Sanskrit _genitive_ and _dative_ **te**; the origin of σφῶϊ is doubtful; with ὑμεῖς cf. Sanskrit _accusative_ _plural_ **yusmān.**)

4573:
** σεβ-άζομαι**,
_Epic dialect_ _aorist_ σεβάσσατο {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.167, 417; ἐσεβάσθην “Anthologia Graeca” 7.122, NT.Rom.1.25}}:—**to be afraid of**, with _accusative_, σεβάσσατο γὰρ τό γε θυμῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same places]}}
**2** later, ={σέβομαι}, ξεῖνον θανόντα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 550; θεόν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “contra Galilaeos” 354b.}}

4574:
** σέβ-ασμα**, ατος, τό,
**that for which awe is felt, an object of awe** or **worship**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.30, NT.Act.17.23, etc.}}
**II** ={σέβασις}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.1.}}

4575:
** σεβαστός**, ή, όν,
**venerable, reverend, august**, πρᾶγμα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.75}}; θεοί, probably of deified Emperors, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 7.2233 (from Thisbe), compare “SIG” 820.6 (Ephesus, 1st c.AD).}}
**II** = Latin {{NT+1st c.BC+|“Augustus”, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.3.8, 12.8.16, NT.Act.25.21, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.11.4, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.10.9, etc.; Καίσαρος Σ. θεοῦ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 21, compare 17}}; ἐπὶ τοῦ πρώτου Σ. in the time of the first {{Refs|“Emperor”, [prev. author] “Laps.” 18; κατὰ τὸν Σ. μάλιστα [prev. author] “Salt.” 34, etc.}}; _feminine_ Σεβαστή, = {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Augusta”, Wilcken “Chr.” 14 ii 7 (1st c.AD), etc., compareΣεβαστιάς}}; joined with Αὔγουστος, -ούστη, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3770 (from Nicomedia).}}
**II.2** name of month, “Augustus”, in Egyptian calendars,= Thoth{{Refs|“Yale Classical Studies” 2.242}}; in Phrygia and elsewhere, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.536, etc.}}
**II.3** σεβαστή, ἡ (i.e. ἡμέρα), the **Emperor's day**, the day on which his birthday or accession day was celebrated every month, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 658 (Egypt, 1st c.BC), “POxy.” 288.32 (1st c.AD), “PMich.Teb.” 123r iv 30 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**II.4** Σεβαστά, τά,= Σεβαστεῖα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|11, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2810 b. 13 (p.1112) (from Aphrodisias),compare “IG” 3.129, 14.748, “SIG” 1065.5 (Cos, 1st c.AD).}}

4576:
** σέβομαι**,
mostly used in _present_: _imperfect_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.197}}: _future_ σεβήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1381.202 (2nd c.AD)}}: _aorist_ ἐσέφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 164, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254b, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Plotini” 12}}:—**feel awe** or **fear** before God,**feel shame**, οὔ νυ σέβεσθ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.242, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 293; τιμῶν καὶ σεβόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 729c}}; σεφθεῖσα **awe-stricken**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.”}} [same place]: rarely with _infinitive_, **dread** or **fear to do** a thing, σ. προσιδέσθαι.., ἀντία φάσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 694 (Lyric poetry); μιαίνειν τὸ θεῖον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69d; σέβεται καὶ φοβεῖται.. τό τι κινεῖν τῶν καθεστώτων [prev. author] “Lg.” 798b}}: so with _accusative_ _of things_, **to fear to do** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.12}}: with _participle_, σ. προσορῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250e.}}
**2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, with _accusative_ _person_, **revere, worship**, Κρονίδαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.25; θεούς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 921, etc.}}; πάντων ἀνάκτων κοινοβωμίαν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 223; Λατώ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 123}}; Λυκοῦργον σέβεσθαι **worship** him **as a hero**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.66, compare 7.197; προσορῶν ὡς θεὸν σ. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251a}}; **do homage to** Zeus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 937}}: generally, **pay honour** or **respect to.. **, θνατοὺς ἄγαν σ. {{Refs|[prev. work] 543 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ φίλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 187 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ph.” 1163 (Lyric poetry), etc.; σ. τινὰ τύχης μάκαρος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 648.}}
**2.b** especially of Jewish proselytes, σεβομένη τὸν θεόν {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Act.16.14, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.7.2}}; σεβόμενοι προσήλυτοι, Ἕλληνες, {{NT|NT.Act.13.43, 17.4; σεβόμεναι γυναῖκες [prev. work] 13.50.}}
**3** of things, τὰ βυβλία σεβόμενοι μεγάλως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.128; ὄργια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 949; ὦ Πιερία, σέβεταί σ᾽ Εὔιος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 566 (Lyric poetry); τὸ σῶφρον αἰδούμενος ἅμα καὶ σ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 837c.}}
**II** _active_ σέβω is post-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, used only in _present_ and _imperfect_, **worship, honour**, mostly of the gods, σ. Δήμητρος πανήγυριν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 120; πατρὸς Ὀλυμπίοιοτιμάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 14.12; θεούς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 596; Νύμφας [prev. author] “Eu.” 22; Ἅιδην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 777}}; τἀν Ἅιδου{{Refs|[prev. work] 780; θεῶν θέσμια [prev. author] “Aj.” 713 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; rare in Prose, νομίζεται θεοὺς σέβειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 600}}; but also of parents, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1377, compare “Ant.” 511}}; of kings, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 667, etc.}}; of suppliants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 151 (Lyric poetry); λέγω κατ᾽ ἄνδρα, μὴ θεόν, σέβειν ἐμέ [prev. author] “Ag.” 925; αἰχμὴν.. μᾶλλον θεοῦ σ. [prev. author] “Th.” 530; σ. ὀνείρων φάσματα [prev. author] “Ag.” 274; τὰς ἐμὰς ἀρχὰς σ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 744 (εὖ σέβουσι is uncertain conjecture for εὐσεβοῦσι in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 338, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1320, “Tr.” 85)}}; σέβειν ἐν τιμῇ with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 166, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 647a: with}}_infinitive_, ὑβρίζειν ἐν κακοῖσιν οὐ σέβω, i.e. τὸ ὑβρίζειν, I do not **respect, approve** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1612; τὸ μὴ ἀδικεῖν σέβοντες [prev. author] “Eu.” 749}}: rarely of a god, Ποσειδῶν.. τὰς ἐμὰς ἀρὰς σέβων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 896:}} —σέβομαι as _passive_, **to be reverenced**, ἡ δ᾽ οἴκοι [πόλις] πλέον δίκῃ σέβοιτ᾽ ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 760}}; τὸ σεβόμενον **reverence**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1101d.}}
**II.2** less frequently _absolutely_, **to worship, to be religious**, τὸν σέβοντ᾽ εὐεργετεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 725, compare 897; οὐ γὰρ σέβεις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 745; κρίνοντες ἐν ὁμοίῳ καὶ σέβειν καὶ μή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53}}; but in all these places an object should perhaps be supplied from the context. (σέβομαι probably originally '**I shrink from.. **', of which σοβέω is the causal; perhaps _cognate_ with Sanskrit _tyajati_ 'desert, let go'.)

4577:
** σειρά**,
_Ionic dialect_ **σειρή**, _Doric dialect_ **σηρά** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus in “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 2.260, “Etymologicum Gudianum” 497.47}}: ἡ:— **cord, rope**, σειράς τ᾽ εὐπλέκτους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.115; σειρὴν δὲ πλεκτήν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.175}}; σ. χρυσείη **cord** of gold, {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153c: _metaphorically_, σειραῖς.. ἁμαρτιῶν σφίγγεται LXX.Pro.5.22}}; σειραὶ ζόφου variant in {{NT|NT.2Pet.2.4: see also σαύρα IV.}}
**2** **trace** (compare σειραφόρος), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.141.}}
**3** **cord** or **line with a noose, lasso**, used by the Sagartians and Sarmatians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.85, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.21.5: hence the Parthians are called σειραφόροι, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**4** a bandage, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).777}}; σ. μονομερής, διμερής, κτλ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 23, 24, +others}}
**5** ἡ σ. τοῦ βίρρου,= **cimussatio** (probably **edge, border**), {{Refs|Dosith. p.435K.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ of an animal's **tail**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 119, 385.}}
**III** σειραὶ τῆς κεφαλῆς **locks** of hair, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Judg.16.13; σ. τριχῶν 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.30.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, **line, lineage**, Tz.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 481, Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.176.}}
**V** **series**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 45, 95.}}
**VI** _plural_, **a disease of horses,** {{Refs|Hippiatr. 52.}}
**VII** **the front part of the perineum**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 6.34.}}

4578:
** σεισμ-ός**, ὁ,
(σείω) **shaking, shock**, γῆς σ. **earthquake**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 862, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.87; χθονός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1166: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28, 5.85, 7.129, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 95, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 791, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23, etc.}}
**2** generally, **shock, agitation, commotion**, σ. τοῦ σώματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 33e, compare “Ti.” 88d}}; ἔξωθεν.. προσφέρειν τοῖς.. πάθεσι σεισμόν **a shock**, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 791a; σ. τῆς οὐρᾶς 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.61; σ. ἐν τῇ θαλάσσῃ NT.Matt.8.24.}}
**3** **blackmail, extortion,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Sammelb. 5675.13 (2nd c.BC)}}; συκοφάντεια καὶ σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 15.67 (2nd c.BC).}}

4579:
** σείω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.563, etc.}}, _poetry_ **σίω** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 49}}: Ep _imperfect_ σεῖον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.486}}; _Ionic dialect_ σείασκον (ἀνασ-) [{{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 403 (variant{ἀνασσείσασκε})}}]: _future_ σείσω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Isa.10.14, (δια-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, (ἐπι-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 613}}: _aorist_ ἔσεισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 713, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 12, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ σεῖσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.321:}}_perfect_ σέσεικα (κατα-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 84, (ἐν-) 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 30:}} —_middle_, _aorist_ ἐσεισάμην (ἀπ-) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 348, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 287, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 484a}}; _Epic dialect_ σείσατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.199, ἐσείσατο 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 1, etc.}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐσείσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.98, etc.}}: _perfect_ σέσεισμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.94, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1276}}:—**shake, move to and fro**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (especially in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”)}}; σ. ἐγχείας, ἔγχεα, μελίην, **shake the poised** spear, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.345, 13.135 (_passive_), 22.133, etc.}}; αἰγίδα {{Refs|15.321}}; σανίδας σ. **shake** the door, {{Refs|9.583}}; of chariot horses, σεῖον ζυγὸν ἀμφὶς ἔχοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.486}}; σ. λόφον, of a warrior, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 22, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 385}}; ἡνίας χεροῖν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 713; χαλινούς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 151 (_anapaest meter_)}}; σ. χαίτην, etc., {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 49, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 75 (Lyric poetry), “Med.” 1191; εὔπτερον δέμας [prev. author] “Ion” 1204}}; κάρα σ., as sign of discontent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 291}}; but of one dancing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 185}}; ἄκρᾳ τῇ οὐρᾷ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.4.}}
**2** of earthquakes, which were attributed to Poseidon {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 403a), ὅστις νομίζει Ποσειδέωνα τὴν γῆν σείειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.129; without τὴν γῆν, αὐτοῖς ὁ Ποσειδῶν σείσας ἐμβάλοι οἰκίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 511, compare “Lys.” 1142}}; βρονταῖς χθόνα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Av.” 1752; ἔσεισεν ὁ θεός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.7.4: also _impersonal_, ἔσεισεν}} **there was an earthquake**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.52.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **agitate, disturb**, πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.272; τὰ πόλεος.. θεοὶ πολλῷ σάλῳ σείσαντες ὤρθωσαν πάλιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 163}}; σ. τὴν καρδίαν **turn** the stomach, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 12}}; σ. τὴν κεφαλήν **cause a concussion of.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.143, see below 11.2}}:—_passive_, ἐσείσθη τὴν καρδίαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.1.11.}}
**4** in _Attic dialect_, **accuse falsely** or **spitefully**, so as to extort hushmoney, **blackmail**, σ. καὶ ταράττων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 840, compare 5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 2; ἔσειον τοὺς παχεῖς καὶ πλουσίους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 639}}; ἑτέρους τῶν ὑπευθύνων ἔσειε καὶ ἐσυκοφάντει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.43, compare “BGU” 428.9 (2nd c.AD)}}; so perhaps σείειν κατ᾽ ἀγοράν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.70 (assuming variant)}}:—_passive_, **to be extorted,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 1252r.37 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** _passive_, **shake, heave, quake**, of the earth, ἐσσείοντο πόδες Ἴδης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.59; Δῆλος.. πρῶτα καὶ ὕστατα.. σεισθεῖσα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.98}}: _metaphorically_, **to be shaken to its foundation**, τὸ τερπνὸν πιτνεῖ.. σεσεισμένον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.94; οἷς.. ἂν σεισθῇ θεόθεν δόμος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 584 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** generally, **move to and fro**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.285; φαεινὴ σείετο πήληξ 13.805; κόμαι σείονται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1312}}; ὄρχος σειόμενος φύλλοισι an orchard **waving** with foliage, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” [299]}}; ὀδόντων οἱ πλεῦνες ἐσείοντο his teeth **were loose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.107; σεισθῆναι σάλῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 46; τὸν ἐγκέφαλον σεσεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1276; ὁκόσων ἂν σεισθῇ ὁ ἐγκέφαλος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.58; σείεσθαι τὴν ὄψιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Vertigine” 8.}}
**III** _middle_, **shake** something **of one's own, from oneself**, etc., σεισαμένας πτερὰ ματρός {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.13; σ. γυίων ἄπο νήχυτον ἅλμην 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1367; σ. πλοκαμῖδας “Anthologia Graeca” 5.272 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**III.2** **shake oneself, shake, stir**, Ἥρη σείσατο εἰνὶ θρόνῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.199; ἐσείσατο δάφνινος ὄρπηξ 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 1.}}

4582:
** σελήν-η**, ἡ,
_Doric dialect_ **σελάνα** [λᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10.75}}, _Aeolic dialect_ **σελάννα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 3,53}}; compare also σεληναίη:— **the moon**, σ. πλήθουσα the full-**moon**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.484}}; σ. ἀεξομένη, ὀλίγη κεράεσσι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 780, 733}}; **a moon's breadth**, measure used by early astronomers, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 9.10}}; νουμηνία κατὰ σελήνην, i.e. by the **lunar** month, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.28, compare “SIG” 683.44 (2nd c.BC)}}; τὰς ἡμέρας κατὰ σ. ἄγειν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.59, compare “PHib.” 1.27.42 (3rd c.BC)}}; πρὸς τὴν σ. ὁρᾶν by **moonlight**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.38, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.9; ἐν σελήνῃ 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 3.2}}; ἡ ἐκ τῆς σ. νόσος,= σεληνιασμός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.27}}; τὴν σ. καθελεῖν, of Thessalian witches, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 750, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 513a, 2nd c.BC: Sosiphanes Tragicus 1.}}
**b** **month**, δεκάτῃ σελήνῃ in the tenth **moon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1126, cf. Alcaeus431, “Tr.” 1075 (Lyric poetry)}}; πολλὰς σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hel.” 114}}; τὰς ἡμέρας τῆς σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” p.3 H.; ὁρῶν ἄγουσαν τὴν σ. εἰκάδας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 17.}}
**2** **a moon-shaped wheaten cake**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 350, compare 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.4, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.76.}}
**3** **a round table**, or **tripod**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.489d.}}
**4** name of a plant, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fluv.” 18.5; compare σελήνιον 11.}}
**II** as _feminine_ _proper name_, **Selene, the goddess of the moon** (never in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 371, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 100, etc. (σελήνη}} (σελάνα, σελάννα) from *σελάσνᾱ, compare σέλας.)

4583:
** σελην-ιάζομαι**,
_passive_, **to be moonstruck**, i.e. **epileptic,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.4.24, 17.15, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 113.10.}}
**2** **to be sublunar**, i.e. subject to change and decay, -ομένης τῆς φύσεως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Zos.Alch. p.107 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, compare “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(3).146.}}

4586:
** σεμνός**,, ή, όν,
(σέβομαι) **revered, august, holy**:
**I** properly of gods, e.g. Demeter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 1, 486; Hecate, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.79; Thetis, [prev. author] “N.” 5.25; Apollo, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 800; Poseidon, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 55; Pallas Athena, [prev. work] 1090 (Lyric poetry)}}; at Athens the Erinyes were specially the σεμναὶ θεαί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 837, “OC” 90, 458, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1312, “Th.” 224, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126, Autocl. cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1398b26}}; or simply Σεμναί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 383 (Lyric poetry), 1041 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 410}}; τὸ σ. ὄνομα their name, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 41; σ. βάθρον the threshold of their temple, [prev. work] 100; σ. τέλη their rites, [prev. work] 1050 (Lyric poetry).}}
**I.2** of things divine, ὄργια σ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 478, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 765; θέμεθλα δίκης 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.14; ὑγίεια 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 70; θυσία 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.42}}; σ. ἄντρον the cave of Cheiron, {{Refs|[prev. author] “P.” 9.30, compare “O.” 5.18}}; σ. δόμος the temple of Apollo, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “N.” 1.72; παιάν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 393}}; σέλμα σ. ἡμένων, of the Olympian gods, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 183 (Lyric poetry)}}; σ. ἔργα, of the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Supp.” 1037 (Lyric poetry); μυστήρια 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 804, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 25; τέρμων οὐρανοῦ [prev. work] 746}}; σ. βίος **devoted to the gods**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 56}}; σεμνὰ φθέγγεσθαι, ={εὔφημα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 109 (variant), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 315, 364}}; ἦ πού τι σ. ἔστιν ὃ ξυναμπέχει; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 521}}; τὸ σ. **holiness**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.126.}}
**II** of human or half-human beings, **reverend, august**, ἐν θρόνῳ σεμνῷ σεμνὸν θωκέοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 975, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 384, +others; σ. θάλος Ἀλκαϊδᾶν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.68; τὸ σχῆμα σεμνὸς κοὐ ταπεινός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 688}}; αἱ φαυλότεραι.. παρὰ τὰς σεμνὰς καθεδοῦνται beside **the great ladies**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 617, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.42; οἱ σεμνότατοι ἐν ταῖς πόλεσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 257d}}; ἄνθρωπος οὐ σ., i.e. a nobody, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 52D.}}; opposed to χαῦνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 227b (_comparative_)}}; opposed to κομψός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.19; σεμνὸς οὐ προσώπου συναγωγαῖς ἀλλὰ βίου κατασκευαῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.44}}: with _dative_, **revered by.. **, σ. πόλει {{Refs|“Riv.Fil.” 57.379 (from Crete)}}; also, **worthy of respect, honourable**, {{NT|NT.1Tim.3.8, 11, NT.Phil.4.8.}}
**II.2** of human things, **august, stately, majestic**, θᾶκοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 519; ἱμάτια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 940, compare “Ra.” 1061 (_comparative_)}}; ταφή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.1}}; πράγματα, ἔργα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1472, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.213; σεμνοτέραν τὴν πόλιν ποιῆσαι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.45; οἰκία τοῦ γείτονος οὐδὲν σεμνοτέρα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.26, compare 29; ψεύδεσι [τοῦ Ὁμήρου] σ. ἔπεστί τι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.22; λεγόντων.. περὶ αὐτοῦ σ. λόγους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; of Tragedy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 502b}}; of style, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1458a21, compare “Rh.” 1404b8}} (_comparative_); of certain metres, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1408b32}}; ἐπὶ τὸ σ. μιμεῖσθαι to imitate it in its **noble qualities**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 814e}}; σ. τι λέγειν, πράσσειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 382b, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 447; σεμνὰ ἄττα μεμαθηκότας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 342a}}; οὐδὲν σ. nothing **very wonderful**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1146a15}}; so τί ἂν εἴη τὸ σ. (i.e. τοῦ νοῦ); {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1074b18}}; **worthy of respect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 996}}; σεμνόν ἐστι, with _infinitive_, 'tis a **noble, fine** thing to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 392a, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 9.5.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, σ. βρῶμα a **noble** dish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 7, compare 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 20; σ. ὀσμή 4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.60, etc.}}
**III** in bad sense, **proud, haughty**, τὰ σέμν᾽ ἔπη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1107; σεμνότερος καὶ φοβερώτερος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.18}}; τὸ σ. **haughty reserve**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 93, compare “Med.” 216.}}
**III.2** in contempt or irony, **solemn, pompous**, σ. καὶ ἅγιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 249a}}; τί σεμνὸν καὶ πεφροντικὸς βλέπει; look **grave and solemn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 773; τὸ σ. ἄγαν καὶ τραγικόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1406b7: very frequently in Comedy texts, ἀνελκτοῖς ὀφρύσι σεμνός 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 355}}; ὡς σ. οὑπίτριπτος how **grand** the rascal is! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 275; ὡς σ. ὁ κατάρατος [prev. author] “Ra.” 178}}; λόγοι σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” 1175; σεμνὸς σεμνῶς χλανίδ᾽ ἕλκων 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 19.}}
**IV** _adverb_ **-νῶς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 193, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1133, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 585, etc.}}; ὥσπερ κοχλίας σεμνῶς ἐπηρκὼς τὰς ὀφρῦς, of Plato, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 13; σεμνῶς κεκοσμημένος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.6, etc.; περὶ εὐτελῶν σ. λέγειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408a13}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.20}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.31.7.}}

4587:
** σεμνό-της**, ητος, ἡ,
**solemnity, dignity**, σεμνότητ᾽ ἔχει σκότος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 486, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.242, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 235b; ἡ σ. τοῦ ῥήματος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 45}}; [τῆς λέξεως] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408b35}}; ἡ τοῦ τόπου σ. {{Refs|“Milet.” 1(9).368}}; also of persons, **seriousness, dignity**, ἐπὶ τῆς σ. αὐθάδεις ὑπολαμβάνεσθαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.14, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1391a28, NT.Tit.2.7, NT.1Tim.2.2: in _plural_, σ. οὐκ ἀληθιναὶ ἀλλὰ πεπλασμέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.98.}}
**II** of a girl, **reserve** or **shyness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1344.}}

4591:
** σημαίνω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.58, etc.}}; **σᾱμαίνω**, {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 686.23 (from Pamphylia)}}: _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ σημαίνεσκον {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 4.193}}: _future_ σημᾰνῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 497, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.20}}, _Ionic dialect_ **-ᾰνέω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.26, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75:}}_aorist_ ἐσήμηνα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 43, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.71}}, _Epic dialect_ σήμηνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.358}}; but in codices of {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus (“HG” 1.1.2, +others) and later writers (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.3.6, NT.Act.25.27, 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 1.41.3, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.2) ἐσήμᾱνα, and so in Mitteis “Chr.” 29.8 (2nd c.BC)}}: _perfect_ σεσήμαγκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristobulus Historicus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 13.12, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.26.29:}} —_middle_, _future_ σημᾰνοῦμαι, _Ionic dialect_ **-έομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 3, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐσημηνάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.175, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ σημανθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.267, (ἐπι-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1593}}: _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 44}}: _aorist_ ἐσημάνθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.16}}: _perfect_ σεσήμασμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.39, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954a, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ σεσήμανται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.125}}, _infinitive_ σεσημάνθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1196}}: (σῆμα):—**show by a sign, indicate, point out**, τέρματα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.358; δείξω ὁδὸν ἠδὲ ἕκαστα σημανέω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.26}}; τοῦτον σημήνας **after indicating** the person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5; τέκμαρ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 667; θησαύρισμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 37; σ. τι περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 682a; σ. ὅ τι χρὴ ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 12}}; σ. εὔδια πάντα (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.22:}} —_middle_, πάντα σημαίνει **you have** all things **shown you** (?), {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 1039.11 (from Limyra).}}
**2** _absolutely_, **give signs**, φθόγγος, φῶς σ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 245, “Ag.” 293; ὁ λόγος σημαινέτω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 345}}; σ. καπνῷ **make signal**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 497: frequently in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} in _future_ with αὐτός, πλοῦς αὐτὸς σημανεῖ {{Refs|“Hel.” 151; τὸ δ᾽ ἔργον αὐτὸ σημανεῖ “Andr.” 265}}; αὐτὸ σημανεῖ (without _substantive_) {{Refs|“Ph.” 623; τἄλλα δ᾽ αὐτὸ σημανεῖ “Ba.” 976.}}
**3** of the Delphic oracle, οὔτε λέγει οὔτε κρύπτει ἀλλὰ σημαίνει {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 93}}; so of omens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.2, etc.; σ. ἐν τοῖς ἱεροῖς [prev. author] “An.” 6.1.31; περί τινος [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.1.19; ἐπὶ τοῖς μέλλουσι γενήσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8; πρὸ τῶν μελλόντων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.17}}:—_passive_, σημαίνεσθαι διὰ τῶν ἐμπύρων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.222f, etc.}}
**4** in later Prose _intransitive_, **appear, be manifest**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 533a11}} (but _passive_ in same sense,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 588b18); σ. ἐκ τῶν εἰρημένων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 989a; compare δηλόω 11.}}
**4.b** σημαίνει _impersonal_, **signs appear**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 941b2, 944a4.}}
**II** **give a sign** or **signal to do** a thing, or **bid** one do it, with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116, 6.78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 26, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 688, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.24}}; **give orders to, bear command over**, with _dative_, πᾶσι δὲ σημαίνειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.289, compare 10.58, 17.250}}; with _genitive_, στρατοῦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.85; also σ. ἐπὶ δμῳῇσι γυναιξίν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.427}}: _absolutely_, **give orders**, ὁ δὲ σημαίνων ἐπέτελλεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.445, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.450: in}}_participle_, σημαίνων,= σημάντωρ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 704 codices}}
**II.2** in war or battle, **give the signal** of attack, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84, etc.; in full, σ. τῇ σάλπιγγι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.1, 5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 37.3; σ. τῷ κέρατι ὡς ἀναπαύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.4: with _accusative_, σ. ἀναχώρησιν}} **give a signal for** retreat, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.10; ἐπειδὰν ὁ σαλπιγκτὴς σημήνῃ τὸ πολεμικόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.29, compare 4.3.32; τὸ ἀνακλητικόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.236e}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.18, etc.: _impersonal_ σημαίνει (i.e. ὁ σαλπιγκτής)}}, **signal is given**, as τοῖσι Ἕλλησι ὡς ἐσήμηνε when **signal was given** for the Greeks to attack, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.11: with}}_infinitive_, ἐσήμαινε παραρτέεσθαι πάντα **signal was given** to make all ready, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.42, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 830}}; also σ. ἐπὶ πλόον πῦρ **gives the signal** for sailing, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 145.}}
**II.3** generally, σ. [τῷ ἵππῳ] τι, προχωρεῖν σ. τῷ ἵππῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.4, 7.10.}}
**II.4** **make signals**, εἰς τὴν πόλιν {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 6.2.33; σ. ὡς πολεμίων ἐπιόντων [prev. work] 7.2.5:}} —_passive_, ἐσημάνθησαν προσπλέουσαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 6.2.34: _absolutely_, σημανθέντων τῷ Ἀστυάγει ὅτι.. [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.4.18.}}
**III** **signify, indicate, declare**, φόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1218; τινί τι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.18, 9.49, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 226: followed by ὡς.., 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34}}; by ὅτι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 320, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62c; σ. ὅστις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 618; σ. ὅ τι χρή σοι συμπράσσειν [prev. work] 297 (_anapaest meter_); σ. ὅπῃ γῆς πεπλάνημαι [prev. work] 564 (_anapaest meter_)}}; σ. ὅπου.. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1294; σ. ὅτου τ᾽ εἶ χὠπόθεν [prev. author] “fragment” 104; σ. εἴτε.. [prev. author] “Ph.” 22; σ. ποίῳ θανάτῳ.. NT.John.12.33, 21.19}}: with _participle_, **signify that** a thing is, φρυκτοῦ φῶς.. σημαίνει μολόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 293; Κρέοντα προσστείχοντα σημαίνουσί μοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 79, compare “OC” 1669; ταῦτα ὡς πολέμου ὄντος σημαίνει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 626e, compare 722e:}} —_passive_, ὁ σημαινόμενος δοῦλος the **abovementioned** slave, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 283.12 (1st c.AD): _absolutely_, σημανθέντος}}**it having been reported,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PAmh. 2.31.8 (2nd c.BC), compare above 11.4.}}
**III.2** **interpret, explain**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108}}; **tell, speak**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.106: _absolutely_, σήμαινε}} **tell**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 51, compare “OT” 1050; οὐ στηλῶν μόνον σ. ἐπιγραφή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43.}}
**III.3** of a writer, **signify, indicate**, ὅτι.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.5}}; of words, sentences, etc., **signify, mean**, ταὐτὸν σημαίνει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 393a, compare 437c, “Phdr.” 275d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 213b30, etc.}}; σημαίνοντα **significant sounds**, opposed to ἄσημα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1457a32f:}} —_passive_, τὸ σημαινόμενον the **sense, meaning** of words, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1405b8, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 31, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 12.27, +others}}; opposed to τὸ σημαῖνον, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.38 (_plural_).}}
**B** _middle_ σημαίνομαι, **give oneself a token**, i.e. **conclude from signs, conjecture**, τὰ μὲν σημαίνομαι, τὰ δ᾽ ἐκπέπληγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 32}}; ἄστροις σ. [τὰς πόλεις], proverbial in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.48; σ. τι ἔκ τινος [prev. work] 2.7}}; of dogs hunting, μυξωτῆρσι σ. τι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.454.}}
**B.II** **provide with a sign** or **mark, seal**, σημαίνεσθαι βύβλῳ (i.e. βοῦν), i.e. by sealing a strip of byblus round his horn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.17, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.1, 2, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 8:}} —_passive_, εὖ σεσημάνθαι **to be** well **sealed up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1196}}; τὰ σεσημασμένα, opposed to τὰ ἀσήμαντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954a, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.7, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.17, “Ath.Mitt.” 7.368.}}
**B.II.2** **mark out, choose for oneself**, τοὺς εὐρωστοτάτους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.71.7.}}

4592:
** σημεῖον**, τό,
_Ionic dialect_ **σημήϊον**, _Doric dialect_ **σᾱμήϊον** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(3).452 (Thera, 4th c.BC)}}, **σᾱμεῖον** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IPE” 12.352.25 (Chersonesus, 2nd c.BC), “IG” 5(1).1390.16 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}, **σᾱμᾶον** {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 5168 (from Cyrene):}} —= σῆμα in all senses, and more common in Prose, but never in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus or 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}}: **mark** by which a thing is known, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38; σημεῖα τῶν δεδικασμένων.., σημεῖα πάντων ὧν ἔπραξαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 614c}}; **sign** of the future, τυραννίδος σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1355; σ. λαβεῖν ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 514}}; **trace, track**, σημεῖα δ᾽ οὔτε θηρὸς οὔτε του κυνῶν.. ἐξεφαίνετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 257, compare “El.” 886; τῆς καταβάσεως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.2.2}}; of a cork on a buoy, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.12.1.}}
**b** _Doric dialect_, **tomb,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(3).452 (4th c.BC), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” [prev. cited]}}
**2** **sign from the gods, omen**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 94; τὰ ἀπὸ τῶν θεῶν σ. γενόμενα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.81, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 244c, “Ap.” 40b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.1}}; **wonder, portent**, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Exo.4.8, +others; σ. καὶ τέρατα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.112.8, NT.Matt.24.24, NT.John.4.48, compare “IPE” [same place], 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.114; φόβηθρα καὶ σ. ἀπ᾽ οὐρανοῦ NT.Luke.21.11}}; especially of **the constellations**, regarded as **signs**, δύεται σημεῖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 529 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ion” 1157.}}
**3** **sign** or **signal** to do a thing, made by flags, ἀνέδεξε σημήϊον τοῖσι ἄλλοισι ἀνάγεσθαι he made **signal** for the rest to put to sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.128}}; **signal for battle**, τὰ σ. ἤρθη, κατεσπάσθη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.49, 63, etc.}}; καθαιρεῖν τὸ σ. to take **it** down, strike the **flag**, as a **sign** of dissolving an assembly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.36}}; τὸ τῆς ἐκκλησίας σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 278}}; ὕστερος ἐλθεῖν τοῦ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 690}}: generally, **signal**, σ. ὑποδηλῶσαί τινι ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 1011; τὰ σ. αὐτοῖς ἤρθη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.42; τὸ σ. τοῦ πυρός, ὡς εἴρητο, ἀνέσχον [prev. work] 111}}; **signal** to commence work, [ἡ] τοῦ σημείου ἄρσις {{Refs|“Ath.Mitt.” 35.403 (Pergam.)}}; σημείῳ ἀβαστάκτῳ, σημείοις ἀβαστάκτοις with unremoved **signal** (**s**), of gymnasia, i.e. never closed, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IGRom.” 4.446 ([prev. passage]), “Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1932(5).44([prev. passage], 2nd c.AD).}}
**4** **standard** or **flag**, on the admiral's ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.92}}; on the general's tent, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.13}}; ἔξω τῶν σ. out of the **lines**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 8.3.19.}}
**4.b** **body of troops** under one standard or flag, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PAmh.” 2.39.2 (2nd c.BC);compare σημεία 1.2.}}
**5** land**mark, boundary, limit**, ἔξω τῶν σ. τοῦ ὑμετέρου ἐμπορίου out of the **limits** of your commercial port, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.28}}; of milestones, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 7, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.13.9.}}
**6** **device** upon a shield, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.171, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1114}}; upon ships, **figure-head**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 933, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 255 (Lyric poetry).}}
**7** **signet** on ring, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 952, “V.” 585, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 191d, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.30, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.2, “PRev.Laws” 26.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; **figure, image**, Διὸς κτησίου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Anticlides Historicus 13}}; **badge**, τρίαιναν σ. θεοῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 218}}: _plural_, **written characters**, γράψαι σημήϊα.. φωνῆς {{Refs|“IG” 14.1549 (from Rome).}}
**7.b** _plural_ (_Doric dialect_) σαμεῖα, **stripes**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work]5(1).1390.16 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; **clavi**· σημεῖα, {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**8** **watchword, war-cry**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.69.8; ἀπὸ σ. ἑνὸς ἐπιστρέφειν τὰς ναῦς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.28.}}
**9** **birthmark** or **distinguishing feature**, Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 76.14 (2nd c.AD), “Sammelb.” 15.27 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **sign, token, indication** of anything that is or is to be, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1059, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1332; σ. φαίνεις ἐσθλὸς.. γεγώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 24, compare “OT” 710; τέχνης σ. τῆς ἐμῆς [prev. author] “Ant.” 998; so later τὰ σ. τῶν καιρῶν NT.Matt.16.3, etc.}}
**II.2** in reasoning, a **sign** or **proof**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 369, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6, 10, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.25, etc.; τούτων ὑμῖν σημεῖα δείξω 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.103, compare 3.46; τάδε τὰ σ. ὡς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.5; σ. εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 520e}}; ὅτι ἀγαθὸς ἦν.., τοῦτο μέγιστον σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Min.” 321b}}; τὸ μὴ ἐκδυθῆναι οὐδὲν σ. ἐστι is no **proof** to the contrary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.5}}; also, **instance, example**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 20}}; σημεῖον δέ· to introduce an argument, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.149, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.86,107, etc.}}
**II.3** in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, **a sign** used as **a probable argument** in proof of a conclusion, opposed to τεκμήριον (a demonstrative or certain proof), {{Refs|“APr.” 70a11, “SE” 167b9, “Rh.” 1357a33.}}
**II.3.b** in Stoic and Epicurean philosophical, **sign** as observable basis of inference to the unobserved or unobservable, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.43U., 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 27, +others, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.142, +others}}; περὶ σημείων (uncertain meaning), title of work by Zeno, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 1.14.}}
**II.4** in Medicine texts, **symptom**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 3.6, 15, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.9, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.313, 18(2).306.}}
**II.4.b** = Latin _lenticula_, a kind of skin-eruption, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Celsus Medicus 6.5.1.}}
**II.5** _plural_, **shorthand symbols**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 23, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἰδίων βιβλίων” 1, “POxy.” 724.3 (2nd c.AD), 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 42.25.}}
**II.6** **critical mark**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus “Poëm.” p.73C., 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 3.65.}}
**III** ={στιγμή}, **mathematical point**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 76b5, “Ph.” 240b3, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra “Def.” 1, +others}}; also ς. (with or without χρόνου) **point** of time, **instant**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 283a11, “Ph.” 262b2f}}
**III.2** in Prosody and Music, **unit of time**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.14, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor “Proll. Heph.” 5.}}

4593:
** σημει-όω**
(_Doric dialect_ **σᾱμ-** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.71 (Andania, 1st c.BC)),= σημαίνω}}, **mark** (by milestones), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.39.8 (_passive_), compare 1.47.1}}; **seal**, ἐπιστολὰς -ωμένας σφραγῖδι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.57}}:—_passive_, **to be marked** or **stamped,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae [prev. cited]}}
**2** **give a signal**, _impersonal_ in _passive_, ὅταν σημειωθῇ Aen. Tact.22.23 (codex M).
**II** _middle_, **mark for oneself, note down**, ὅτι.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.21.7; τόπον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.28.9: _absolutely_, Δυμᾶς σεσημείωμαι}} (**signed**) Δυμᾶς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Ostr.Bodl.” iii 280 (1st c.AD), compare ii 25 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; **take notice of, pay honour to.. **, ὅπως ὁ δῆμος φαίνηται τοὺς καλοὺς κἀγαθοὺς τῶν ἀνδρῶν σημειούμενος {{Refs|“Inscription Perg.” 252.28.}}
**II.2** **interpret** anything **as a sign** or **portent**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.78.2, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.2.11}}; **infer as from a sign**, ὁ ἐκ τῆς ἐναργείας -ούμενος περὶ τῶν ἀδήλων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 15, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.47U., 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.271}}:—in Medicine texts, **diagnose**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.2.1, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).851}}; later, **examine**, σημειωσόμεθα κοπαρίῳ {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.77; οἱ τοῦ -ουμένου δάκτυλοι [prev. work] 96.}}
**II.3** in Grammars, of marginal marks, σημείωσαι,= **nota bene**, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.87, +others, frequently in Scholia}}:—in _passive_, σημειοῦται δὲ ὅτι.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” “Oxy.” 1086i17}}; τὰ σεσημειωμένα **noted as exceptions**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 115.11, 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.406}} H.: _future_ σεσημειώσεται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 166.14.}}

4595:
** σήπω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 995, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 84d, etc.}}: _future_ σήψω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 275:}}_aorist_ ἔσηψα (δι-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 9.62}}:—**make rotten** or **putrid**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “fragment” l}}.with; of a serpent's poison, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ch.” l}}.with; of the sting of the σήψ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 16.40.}}
**b** **soak** hides, δέρματα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.18 (Athens, 5th c.BC).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **corrupt, waste**, αἱ ἡσυχίαι σήπουσι καὶ ἀπολλύασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153c; σ. τὰ τῆς πόλεως πράγματα 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.37.}}
**II** mostly in _passive_ (_perfect_ σέσηπα being used in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.135, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 319, (κατα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1035, (ἀπο-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.12)}}, _future_ σᾰπήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.397, (κατα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86b, (ἀπο-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.1:}}_aorist_ ἐσάπην [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 152, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.41, 3.66, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 80d}}; σαπήῃ (κατα-), _Epic dialect_ _subjunctive_ for σαπῇ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.27:}}_perfect_ σέσημμαι probably in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1449.51 (3rd c.AD)}}:—**rot, moulder**, of dead bodies, χρὼς σήπεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.414, compare 19.27, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.41; περὶ ῥινοῖο σαπείσης 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 152}}; of wood, δοῦρα σέσηπε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.135; τριήρης ὑπὸ τερηδόνων σαπεῖσα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1308.}}
**II.2** of live flesh, **mortify**, ὁ μηρὸς ἐσάπη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.66; σφακελίσαντος τοῦ μηροῦ καὶ σαπέντος [prev. author] 6.136, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 80d; αἷμα σέσηπεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 319}}; **promote coction** or formation of 'laudable' pus, in _active_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.6, 28.}}
**II.3** of water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aër.” 8; οἶνος.. σαπὲν ἐν ξύλῳ ὕδωρ 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 81.}}
**II.4** of the food rejected after digestion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 381b12, +others; compare σηπτός, σῆψις 11.}}
**II.5** _metaphorically_, σ. ὑπὸ τῆς ἡδονῆς {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 23; ὁ πλοῦτος ὑμῶν σέσηπε NT.Jam.5.2.}}

4596:
** σηρῐκός**, ή, όν,
(Σήρ) **Seric, silken**, ἐσθής {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 63}}; παραπετάσματα, σκευή, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 43.24, 59.26; νῆμα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.942 (_plural_), 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.31; τὰ σ. τῶν ὑφασμάτων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.396b}}; written **σειρικός**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.46}}:—as _substantive_, **σηρικόν**, τό, **silken robe, silk,** [{{NT+1st c.BC+|NT.Rev. 18.12 (variant{σιρικόν}), “Peripl.M. Rubr.” 49; in _plural_, Nearchus Historicus cited in 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.20.}}]
**2** σηρικά, τά, **jujubes**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.614, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 1.81.}}
**3** σηρικόν (perhaps συρικόν), τό, a **red pigment**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Olymp.Alch. p.76 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, 3rd-4th c.AD: Zosimus Alchemista p.248 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}; **Syricum pigmentum, quod Syrii Phoenices in Rubri maris litoribus colligunt**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th-7th c.AD: Isidorus Hispalensis Grammaticus “Etym.” 19.17.6 (where it is distinguished from “Sericum”).}}

4597:
** σής**, ὁ,
_genitive_ σεός (as if from σεύς, which is given as _nominative_ by {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1p.406H.); _plural_, _nominative_ σέες Moer. p.339 P.}}; _genitive_ σέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 94, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 730, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.461 (not σεῶν, see 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus [same place]); _accusative_ σέας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 1:}} —the forms σητός, σῆτες, σητῶν, etc., were later, as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 540.5, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 557b3, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.11, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.361 (and variant in 2.461), compare 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus}} [same place], etc.:—**moth**, [χρυσὸν] οὐ σ. οὐδὲ κὶς δάπτει {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 222, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus [same place], 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.54, NT.Matt.6.19, NT.Luke.12.33; attacking books, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista [prev. cited]}}
**2** _metaphorically_, σῆτες ἀκανθοβάται or -λόγοι, of the Grammarians, **bookworms,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 11.322 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus), 347 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
